Let $p(x)=(x+3)(x-2)(x-5)$. The zeros are $-3$, $2$ and $5$. The positive leading coefficient gives a rising right-hand branch, and the curve crosses the axis at each simple root.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-4.3,0)--(6.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1.5)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-4:6,samples=160] plot (\x,{(\x+3)*(\x-2)*(\x-5)/30});
\foreach \r in {-3,2,5} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is above the $x$-axis for $-3<x<2$ or $x>5$. Exclude all three zeros because the inequality is strict.
$(-3,2)\cup(5,\infty)$

Let $p(x)=(x+1)^2(x-2)$. The curve touches the axis at the double root $-1$ and crosses at the simple root $2$. Its right-hand branch rises.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-2.4,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.1)--(0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-2.2:3,samples=160] plot (\x,{(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x-2)/8});
\foreach \r in {-1,2} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is below the axis for $x<2$, except at $x=-1$, where it is zero. Exclude both zeros.
$(-\infty,-1)\cup(-1,2)$

Move all terms to the left and factor:
$\begin{align*}x^3-16x&<0\\x(x^2-16)&<0\\x(x-4)(x+4)&<0\end{align*}$
Sketch $y=x(x-4)(x+4)$. The zeros are $-4$, $0$ and $4$; the curve crosses at each zero and its right-hand branch rises.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-5.3,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-5:5,samples=160] plot (\x,{\x*(\x-4)*(\x+4)/24});
\foreach \r in {-4,0,4} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Read where the graph is below the axis. The strict inequality excludes the zeros.
$(-\infty,-4)\cup(0,4)$

Move all terms to the left and factor:
$\begin{align*}x^3-6x^2&\le0\\x^2(x-6)&\le0\end{align*}$
Sketch $y=x^2(x-6)$. The curve touches the axis at $0$ and crosses at $6$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.8,0)--(7.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-1.5:7,samples=160] plot (\x,{(\x)*(\x)*(\x-6)/24});
\foreach \r in {0,6} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is below the axis for $x<6$, apart from its zero at $0$. Include both zeros because equality is allowed.
$(-\infty,6]$

Move all terms to the left and factor:
$\begin{align*}x^3-6x^2+9x&\le0\\x(x^2-6x+9)&\le0\\x(x-3)^2&\le0\end{align*}$
Sketch $y=x(x-3)^2$. The curve crosses the axis at $0$ and touches it at $3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.2,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.2)--(0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-1:4.5,samples=160] plot (\x,{\x*(\x-3)*(\x-3)/8});
\foreach \r in {0,3} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is below the axis for $x<0$. Include $0$ and the isolated zero $3$ because equality is allowed.
$(-\infty,0]\cup\{3\}$

Move all terms to the left and factor by grouping:
$\begin{align*}2x^3-10x^2-8x+40&<0\\2x^2(x-5)-8(x-5)&<0\\2(x-5)(x^2-4)&<0\\2(x-5)(x-2)(x+2)&<0\end{align*}$
Sketch $y=2(x-5)(x-2)(x+2)$. The zeros are $-2$, $2$ and $5$; the curve crosses at each zero and its right-hand branch rises.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3.2,0)--(6.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-3:6,samples=160] plot (\x,{2*(\x-5)*(\x-2)*(\x+2)/40});
\foreach \r in {-2,2,5} {\draw[black] (\r,-0.06)--(\r,0.06);\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\r,0) {$\r$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is below the axis for $x<-2$ or $2<x<5$. Exclude the zeros.
$(-\infty,-2)\cup(2,5)$

Move all terms to the left and factor by grouping:
$\begin{align*}x^3-3x^2+2x-6&\ge0\\x^2(x-3)+2(x-3)&\ge0\\(x-3)(x^2+2)&\ge0\end{align*}$
Since $x^2+2>0$ for every real $x$, the only real zero is $3$. The graph lies below the axis for $x<3$ and above it for $x>3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.3,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1.8)--(0,2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-1:4,samples=160] plot (\x,{(\x-3)*((\x)*(\x)+2)/12});
\draw[black] (3,-0.06)--(3,0.06);
\node[below=2pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (3,0) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Include the zero because equality is allowed.
$[3,\infty)$
